#!/bin/bash
# Setting up Tailscale with Claude (plugin.conf's setup, run in this folder; Welcome to Nightdrive runs it too): Claude
# explains what it is for and logs this machine in by ./login, which opens Tailscale's page in the browser. The account
# and its sign-in stay in the browser.
dir=$(dirname "$(readlink -f "$0")")
"$HOME/.config/hypr/scripts/claude-setup" tailscale Tailscale <<EOF
Log this computer in to Tailscale with me, by $dir/README.md, section "Setting up". First say in a few plain sentences
what Tailscale is for: a private network of my own devices, so I can reach this computer (and my home server, NAS or Home
Assistant) from my phone or laptop wherever I am, and it costs nothing for personal use. Ask whether I already have a
Tailscale account; if not, the login page makes one (with Google, Microsoft, GitHub or Apple), so I only pick one there.
Then run $dir/login: it opens the login page in my browser, and I finish there. Check with \`tailscale status\` until
this computer shows as connected; if login says "Permission denied", give me \`! sudo tailscale set --operator=\$USER\`
to run myself, then run login again. Finish by telling me where it lives now: the Tailscale icon in the top bar (a
right click turns it on or off, its menu lists my devices and copies their addresses), and that my phone needs the
Tailscale app, signed in to the same account.
EOF
